BYLAWS
OF
GREEN RIDGE MAIZE COOPERATIVE
(GRMC)

Adopted: 1st March, 2021
Amended: 15th July, 2023 (as noted herein)

---

PREAMBLE

We, the founding members of Green Ridge Maize Cooperative, do hereby establish these Bylaws to govern the operations, membership, and internal affairs of the Cooperative. The Cooperative is a voluntary association of maize farmers and allied stakeholders in the Green Ridge area of Kaduna State, Nigeria, formed for the purpose of promoting agricultural productivity, collective marketing, and the economic welfare of its members.

The Cooperative shall operate on the principles of self-help, mutual assistance, and democratic control. We affirm our commitment to the cooperative movement and to the sustainable development of our community through honest enterprise and sound stewardship of our resources.

---

ARTICLE I: NAME, REGISTRATION, AND PURPOSE

Section 1.01. Name.
The name of this organization shall be "Green Ridge Maize Cooperative," hereinafter referred to as "the Cooperative." The Cooperative may also be known by its acronym, "GRMC."

Section 1.02. Registration.
The Cooperative was duly registered with the appropriate authority under the Cooperative Societies Law of Kaduna State. The Cooperative Bylaws state that Green Ridge Maize Cooperative has registration_number GRC/2021/045. Record status: current. This registration number is current and remains the official identifier of the Cooperative for all legal and administrative purposes.

Section 1.03. Registered Office.
The registered office of the Cooperative shall be located at the Green Ridge Agricultural Settlement, along the Kachia Road, Chikun Local Government Area, Kaduna State, Nigeria. The Board of Directors may, by resolution, change the registered office address, provided that all members are notified in writing at least thirty (30) days prior to such change taking effect.

Section 1.04. Purpose.
The primary purposes of the Cooperative shall be:

(a) To promote the cultivation, harvesting, processing, and marketing of maize and other related crops among its members;

(b) To provide members with access to affordable inputs, including improved seeds, fertilizers, and agrochemicals, through bulk procurement and distribution;

(c) To facilitate access to credit and financial services for members through partnerships with financial institutions and government agricultural programs;

(d) To provide training and technical assistance to members on modern farming techniques, soil conservation, pest control, and post-harvest handling;

(e) To establish and maintain storage, drying, and processing facilities for the benefit of members;

(f) To collectively market members' produce to secure better prices and reduce transaction costs;

(g) To promote the welfare of members and their families through mutual aid and cooperative social programs;

(h) To liaise with government agencies, research institutions, and non-governmental organizations on matters affecting maize production and rural development;

(i) To engage in any other lawful activity that may be conducive to the attainment of the above purposes.

Section 1.05. Powers.
The Cooperative shall have the power to:

(a) Acquire, hold, lease, sell, or dispose of real and personal property;

(b) Enter into contracts and agreements in the name of the Cooperative;

(c) Borrow money and incur liabilities, as permitted by law and by these Bylaws;

(d) Invest its funds in accordance with the direction of the Board of Directors;

(e) Appoint, employ, or engage staff, agents, and consultants as necessary;

(f) Open and operate bank accounts in the name of the Cooperative;

(g) Sue and be sued in its own name;

(h) Do all such other things as may be incidental to or consequential upon the exercise of its powers.

---

ARTICLE II: MEMBERSHIP

Section 2.01. Eligibility.
Membership in the Cooperative shall be open to any person who:

(a) Is engaged in maize farming or a closely related agricultural activity within the Cooperative's area of operation;

Section 3.04. Duties and Powers of the Board.
The Board shall have general supervision and control of the business and affairs of the Cooperative and shall exercise all powers of the Cooperative not reserved to the general membership by law, by the Articles of Association, or by these Bylaws. Without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the Board shall have the power and duty to:

(a) Employ and supervise a General Manager and such other staff as may be necessary, and fix their compensation and terms of employment;

(b) Determine the prices to be paid for members' produce and the prices to be charged for inputs and services, subject to any policies adopted by the general membership;

(c) Authorize expenditures and incur liabilities on behalf of the Cooperative within the limits of the approved budget;

(d) Approve the annual budget and the annual financial statements for presentation to the general membership;

(e) Arrange for an annual audit of the Cooperative's accounts by a qualified auditor;

(f) Establish committees as may be necessary and delegate to them such powers as the Board deems appropriate, provided that the Board shall remain responsible for the exercise of all delegated powers;

(g) Make and enforce rules and regulations for the conduct of the Cooperative's business, not inconsistent with these Bylaws or with the decisions of the general membership;

(h) Borrow money and pledge the assets of the Cooperative as security, subject to the limitations set out in Section 3.05;

The Cooperative Bylaws specify that the Board of Directors has meeting_frequency_months 3. Record status: current.

(i) Acquire, lease, or dispose of property as the interests of the Cooperative may require;

(j) Carry out such other duties as may be prescribed by law, by these Bylaws, or by resolution of the general membership.

Section 3.05. Limitations on the Powers of the Board.
The Board shall not, without the prior approval of a two-thirds majority of the members present and voting at a general meeting:

(a) Sell, mortgage, or otherwise encumber any real property of the Cooperative;

(b) Borrow money in excess of Five Hundred Thousand Naira (NGN 500,000) in any single transaction or series of related transactions;

(c) Enter into any contract involving an expenditure in excess of Two Hundred Thousand Naira (NGN 200,000) unless such expenditure was included in the approved annual budget;

(d) Change the nature of the Cooperative's business or undertake any activity not reasonably related to the purposes set out in Article I;

(e) Amend, repeal, or adopt any Bylaw, except as provided in Article VI.

Section 3.06. Meetings of the Board.
The Board shall meet at least once every three months, and more often as the business of the Cooperative may require. The Cooperative Bylaws specify that the Board of Directors has meeting_frequency_months 3, and this provision is currently in force as the minimum cadence for regular Board sittings. Meetings shall be called by the Chairperson, or by the Secretary upon the written request of at least three (3) Directors. Notice of each meeting shall be given to each Director at least seven (7) days in advance, either personally, by telephone, or by electronic mail, and shall state the time, place, and agenda of the meeting. The Board may hold meetings by telephone conference or other electronic means, provided that all participants can communicate with one another simultaneously and that the minutes reflect the method of participation. A majority of the Directors then in office shall constitute a quorum for the transaction of business at any meeting of the Board, and the act of a majority of the Directors present at a meeting at which a quorum is present shall be the act of the Board, except as otherwise provided in these Bylaws.

Section 3.07. Special Meetings.
Special meetings of the Board may be called by the Chairperson at any time and shall be called by the Secretary upon the written request of at least three (3) Directors. Special meetings shall be held on not less than forty-eight (48) hours' notice, and the notice shall state the purpose of the meeting. No business shall be transacted at a special meeting other than that stated in the notice, unless all Directors are present and consent to the consideration of additional matters.

Section 4.05. Quorum.
Except as otherwise provided in these Bylaws or by law, the presence in person or by proxy of fifty percent (50%) of the total membership entitled to vote shall constitute a quorum for the transaction of business at any meeting of the members. The Cooperative Bylaws state that the Cooperative Bylaws have quorum_percent 50, and this provision is currently in force as the minimum threshold for convening a valid members' meeting. Once a quorum is present at a meeting, the meeting may continue to transact business even if some members withdraw, so long as the number of members remaining in person or by proxy is not less than one-third (1/3) of the total membership entitled to vote. If a quorum is not present at the time fixed for the meeting, the members present in person or by proxy may adjourn the meeting from time to time without further notice, and a quorum shall be required at any reconvened meeting, except that if the adjournment is for more than thirty (30) days, notice of the adjourned meeting shall be given as provided in Section 4.04.

Section 4.06. Voting Rights.
Each member in good standing shall be entitled to one (1) vote on each matter submitted to a vote of the members. A member in good standing is one who has paid all fees and assessments due and is not suspended under Section 2.08. Voting shall be by voice vote or by show of hands, except that the Chairperson may order a written ballot, or any member may demand a written ballot, on any question. Elections of Directors shall be by written ballot, unless there is only one candidate for each open seat, in which case the Chairperson may declare the candidates elected by acclamation.

Section 4.07. Proxies.
A member entitled to vote may vote in person or by proxy executed in writing by the member or by the member's duly authorized attorney-in-fact. No proxy shall be valid for more than eleven (11) months from the date of its execution, unless the proxy states a longer period, not exceeding three (3) years. A proxy shall be revocable at the pleasure of the member executing it, and the attendance in person at a meeting of the member executing the proxy shall revoke the proxy for that meeting, unless the proxy expressly states that it is coupled with an interest. Each proxy shall be filed with the Secretary before the time fixed for the meeting at which it is to be used. The Board may adopt rules governing the form and use of proxies, provided that such rules are consistent with these Bylaws.

The Cooperative Bylaws state that the Cooperative Bylaws have quorum_percent 50. Record status: current.

Section 4.08. Action by Written Consent.
Any action required or permitted to be taken at a meeting of the members may be taken without a meeting if one or more written consents setting forth the action so taken are signed by members holding not less than the minimum number of votes that would be necessary to authorize or take such action at a meeting at which all members entitled to vote thereon were present and voted. Such consents shall be filed with the Secretary and shall have the same force and effect as a vote at a meeting. The Secretary shall give prompt notice of any action taken by written consent to all members who did not sign the consent, in the manner provided in Section 4.04.

Section 4.09. Adjournments.
Any meeting of the members, whether or not a quorum is present, may be adjourned by the vote of a majority of the members present in person or by proxy. No notice of an adjourned meeting need be given if the time and place to which the meeting is adjourned are announced at the meeting, except as provided in Section 4.05. At any adjourned meeting at which a quorum is present, any business may be transacted that might have been transacted at the original meeting.

Section 4.10. Order of Business.
The order of business at all meetings of the members shall be:
(a) Call to order and determination of a quorum;
(b) Reading and approval of the minutes of the previous meeting;
(c) Reports of officers and committees;
(d) Election of Directors, if any;
(e) Unfinished business;
(f) New business;
(g) Adjournment.

# GREEN RIDGE MAIZE COOPERATIVE BYLAWS

## ARTICLE V: MEMBERSHIP

### Section 5.01. Eligibility for Membership.

Any natural person who is engaged in the production of maize or other grain crops within the cooperative's operational area, or who is directly involved in the agricultural value chain as a supplier of inputs, transporter, or processor, shall be eligible to apply for membership. The Board of Directors shall have the authority to determine, in its sole discretion, whether an applicant's activities are sufficiently connected to the cooperative's purposes to qualify for membership. Persons engaged primarily in speculative trading of grain without any productive or logistical connection to the cooperative's operations shall not be eligible.

Corporate entities, partnerships, and other organizations may apply for membership provided that such entity designates a natural person as its authorized representative for all purposes under these Bylaws. The cooperative may admit no more than one representative of any single entity as a member.

### Section 5.02. Application Process.

Any person seeking membership shall submit a written application to the Secretary on a form prescribed by the Board. The application shall include:

(a) The applicant's full name, address, and contact information;

(b) A statement of the applicant's agricultural activities, including the location and approximate size of any farmland, or the nature of the applicant's connection to the agricultural value chain;

(c) The names of two current members who can attest to the applicant's character and commitment to cooperative principles;

(d) The applicant's agreement to abide by these Bylaws and any policies adopted by the Board;

(e) Such other information as the Board may reasonably require from time to time.

Upon receipt of a complete application, the Secretary shall refer the application to the Membership Committee for review. The Membership Committee shall consist of three members appointed by the Board, one of whom shall be a Director. The Committee shall investigate the application and submit its recommendation to the Board within thirty days of receipt.

The Board shall act on each application at its next regular meeting following the Committee's recommendation. The Board may require the applicant to appear before it to answer questions regarding the application. All applications shall be considered on their merits without regard to gender, ethnicity, religion, or political affiliation.

An applicant shall become a member upon:

(a) Approval of the application by the Board;

(b) Payment of the membership fee, if any, as established by the Board; and

(c) The applicant's execution of a written acknowledgment, in a form prescribed by the Board, confirming the applicant's agreement to be bound by these Bylaws and by any policies adopted by the Board or by the members.

### Section 5.03. Membership Register.

The Secretary shall maintain a register of members containing the name, address, date of admission, and such other information as the Board may require for each member. The register shall be available for inspection by any member during normal business hours at the cooperative's principal office. Any change in a member's name, address, or other contact information shall be communicated promptly to the Secretary in writing.

### Section 5.04. Rights of Members.

Each member shall have the right to:

(a) Attend and participate in all meetings of the members;

(b) Vote on all matters properly brought before the members, subject to the provisions of these Bylaws regarding voting rights;

(c) Hold office in the cooperative, subject to the qualifications and election procedures set forth in these Bylaws;

(d) Receive all services, benefits, and patronage dividends that the cooperative may provide to its members;

